**Sec. 71101**
This section imposes a moratorium until September 30, 2034, preventing the Secretary of Health and Human Services from implementing the CMS final rule titled "Streamlining Medicaid; Medicare Savings Program Eligibility Determination and Enrollment." The prohibition applies specifically to amendments made to five sections of title 42 of the Code of Federal Regulations, including sections 406.21(c) and 435.4. To support this enforcement, $1,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 71102**
The Secretary of Health and Human Services is prohibited from implementing, administering, or enforcing the CMS final rule titled "Medicaid Program; Streamlining the Medicaid, Children's Health Insurance Program, and Basic Health Program Application, Eligibility Determination, Enrollment, and Renewal Processes" until September 30, 2034. This moratorium covers specific amendments to parts 431, 435, 447, and 457 of title 42 of the Code of Federal Regulations, including sections 435.222 and 457.344. The restriction applies to eligibility and enrollment processes for Medicaid, the Children's Health Insurance Program (CHIP), and the Basic Health Program.

**Sec. 71103**
States must establish processes to regularly obtain address information for Medicaid enrollees by January 1, 2027, and submit Social Security numbers and other data to a new federal system by October 1, 2029, to prevent duplicate enrollment across states. The Secretary of Health and Human Services must establish this system to identify individuals enrolled in multiple state plans and require states to disenroll individuals who do not reside in the state claiming them. Managed care entities and prepaid health plans must transmit verified address information to states beginning January 1, 2027. Funding of $10,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and $20,000,000 for fiscal year 2029 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for system establishment and maintenance.

**Sec. 71104**
Beginning January 1, 2027, states must screen Medicaid enrollees against the Death Master File at least quarterly to identify deceased individuals. States are required to treat Death Master File data as factual confirmation of death, immediately disenroll such individuals, and discontinue payments for services furnished after the date of death. If an individual is erroneously disenrolled due to misidentification, the state must immediately re-enroll them with retroactive coverage.

**Sec. 71105**
Beginning January 1, 2028, states must check the Death Master File during provider enrollment, revalidation, and at least quarterly thereafter to determine if a Medicaid provider or supplier is deceased. This requirement amends section 1902(kk)(1) of the Social Security Act to ensure that deceased providers do not remain enrolled in the Medicaid program.

**Sec. 71106**
Effective for fiscal year 2030, this section modifies the calculation of erroneous excess payments under Medicaid by allowing the Secretary to consider audits conducted by states and clarifying that payments with insufficient information to confirm eligibility count as erroneous. The amount of federal funds waived for a fiscal year cannot exceed the erroneous excess payments for medical assistance that exceed the allowable error rate of 0.03 percent. The definition of erroneous payments is expanded to include payments for items and services furnished to ineligible individuals or where eligibility cannot be confirmed.

**Sec. 71107**
Beginning with the first quarter after December 31, 2026, states must conduct eligibility redeterminations every six months for individuals enrolled under the Medicaid expansion category (section 1902(a)(10)(A)(i)(VIII)) or equivalent waiver coverage. This requirement excludes individuals described in section 1902(xx)(9)(A)(ii)(II), which includes certain medically frail or special needs individuals. The Secretary of Health and Human Services must issue implementation guidance within 180 days of enactment, and $75,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 71108**
Effective January 1, 2028, states may elect to apply a home equity limit of up to $1,000,000 for individuals seeking long-term care services, replacing the previous $500,000 limit for non-agricultural homes. For homes on lots zoned for agricultural use, states must apply the standard limit specified in section 1917(f)(1)(A). The section clarifies that states cannot determine eligibility for nursing facility or other long-term care services without applying the home equity limit under section 1917(f)(1).

**Sec. 71109**
Beginning October 1, 2026, federal payments to states for medical assistance are prohibited unless the individual is a resident of the United States and is either a citizen, a lawful permanent resident, a Cuban or Haitian entrant, or a resident under a Compact of Free Association. This restriction applies to Medicaid and CHIP, excluding payments for emergency services under section 2105(a)(1)(D)(ii). $15,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 to implement these changes.

**Sec. 71110**
Beginning October 1, 2026, the Federal Medical Assistance Percentage (FMAP) for emergency medical care furnished to aliens who are not eligible for full Medicaid coverage is capped at the standard state FMAP rate. This provision amends section 1905 of the Social Security Act to limit federal reimbursement for emergency services provided to ineligible aliens. $1,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 for implementation.

**Sec. 71111**
The Secretary of Health and Human Services is prohibited from implementing, administering, or enforcing the CMS final rule titled "Medicare and Medicaid Programs; Minimum Staffing Standards for Long-Term Care Facilities and Medicaid Institutional Payment Transparency Reporting" until September 30, 2034. This moratorium specifically blocks amendments to sections 483.5 and 483.35 of part 483 of title 42 of the Code of Federal Regulations.

**Sec. 71112**
Effective for applications made after the first quarter beginning after December 31, 2026, this section reduces the retroactive coverage period for Medicaid and CHIP. For individuals enrolled under the expansion category, coverage is limited to services furnished in or after the month before the application month, while for other individuals, it is limited to services furnished in or after the second month before the application month. CHIP states electing to provide pre-application coverage must limit it to services furnished before the second month preceding the application month. $10,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 71113**
For one year following enactment, federal direct spending funds for Medicaid cannot be used to pay entities that provide abortions (except in cases of rape, incest, or life endangerment) and received over $800,000 in federal and state Medicaid expenditures in fiscal year 2023. This prohibition applies to 501(c)(3) organizations and essential community providers primarily engaged in family planning and reproductive health. $1,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026 to implement this restriction.

**Sec. 71114**
The increased Federal Medical Assistance Percentage (FMAP) incentive for states that did not expand Medicaid prior to January 1, 2026, is sunsetted. States must begin expending amounts for all eligible individuals prior to January 1, 2026, to qualify for the incentive, effectively ending the eligibility for states that have not yet expanded coverage by that date.

**Sec. 71115**
For fiscal years beginning on or after October 1, 2026, the hold harmless threshold for broad-based health care related taxes is reduced for expansion states, phasing down from 5.5 percent in fiscal year 2028 to 3.5 percent in fiscal year 2032 and subsequent years. Non-expansion states retain their current applicable percent if they have enacted and imposed a tax within the hold harmless threshold as of the date of enactment. The section defines expansion and non-expansion states and applies only to the 50 states and the District of Columbia. $20,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 71116**
The Secretary of Health and Human Services must revise regulations to limit state directed payments to managed care entities to 100 percent of the Medicare payment rate for expansion states and 110 percent for non-expansion states. Payments approved before May 1, 2025, or for rural hospitals approved before enactment, are grandfathered but must be reduced by 10 percentage points annually starting January 1, 2028, until they meet the new limits. $7,000,000 is appropriated for each fiscal year from 2026 through 2033 to carry out these revisions.

**Sec. 71117**
This section clarifies that a Medicaid provider tax is not considered generally redistributive if it imposes lower rates on taxpayers with higher Medicaid taxable units or higher rates on Medicaid taxable units compared to non-Medicaid units. It defines "Medicaid taxable unit" and "non-Medicaid taxable unit" to include bases such as Medicaid bed days, revenue, and costs. The amendments apply to the 50 states and the District of Columbia, effective upon enactment with a transition period not exceeding three fiscal years.

**Sec. 71118**
Beginning January 1, 2027, the Secretary of Health and Human Services cannot approve Medicaid demonstration projects under section 1115 unless the Chief Actuary of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services certifies that the project is budget neutral. Budget neutrality means the project is not expected to increase federal expenditures compared to what would have been spent without the project. $5,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for each of fiscal years 2026 and 2027.

**Sec. 71119**
Beginning the first quarter after December 31, 2026, states must require "applicable individuals" (ages 19-64, not pregnant, not disabled, etc.) to demonstrate community engagement, such as working, volunteering, or studying for at least 80 hours per month, as a condition of Medicaid eligibility. States must provide notice and a 30-day cure period for noncompliance before denying or disenrolling an individual, with exceptions for specified groups like veterans, caregivers, and the medically frail. $200,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026, plus $200,000,000 in grants to states for system implementation.

**Sec. 71120**
Beginning October 1, 2028, states must impose cost sharing on "specified individuals" (Medicaid expansion enrollees with family income above the poverty line) for certain services, excluding primary care, mental health, substance use disorder services, and services from FQHCs or rural health clinics. Cost sharing cannot exceed $35 per item or service, and total annual cost sharing for a family cannot exceed 5 percent of family income. $15,000,000 is appropriated to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 71121**
Beginning July 1, 2028, the Secretary may approve standalone waivers for home or community-based services (HCBS) that do not require individuals to meet the institutional level of care criteria, provided states establish needs-based eligibility criteria. States must attest that per capita expenditures for these individuals will not exceed institutional care costs and must report data on service costs and utilization. $50,000,000 is appropriated for fiscal year 2026 for implementation, and $100,000,000 for fiscal year 2027 for state systems support.

**Sec. 71201**
Individuals are entitled to Medicare benefits only if they are U.S. citizens, lawful permanent residents, Cuban or Haitian entrants, or residents under a Compact of Free Association. For individuals currently enrolled, this restriction applies 18 months after enactment, with the Commissioner of Social Security required to review and notify ineligible individuals within one year.

**Sec. 71202**
The temporary payment increase under the Medicare Physician Fee Schedule is extended to include services furnished in 2026, providing a 2.5 percent increase for that year. This amendment modifies section 1848(t) of the Social Security Act to account for exceptional circumstances in 2026, similar to the adjustments made for 2021 through 2024.

**Sec. 71203**
The exclusion for orphan drugs from the Medicare Drug Price Negotiation Program is expanded to include drugs approved for one or more rare diseases, rather than only one. Additionally, drugs that lose their orphan drug status after approval are treated as if they were never eligible for the exclusion from the date they lost that status. These changes apply to initial price applicability years beginning on or after January 1, 2028.

**Sec. 71301**
Premium tax credits are restricted to individuals who are U.S. citizens or "eligible aliens," defined as lawful permanent residents, Cuban or Haitian entrants, or Compact of Free Association residents. Lawfully present aliens who do not meet these specific criteria are ineligible for the credit. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026.

**Sec. 71302**
The premium tax credit is disallowed for individuals during periods when they are ineligible for Medicaid solely due to their alien status. This amendment removes the provision that previously allowed credits for such individuals, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71303**
Exchanges must verify an individual's eligibility to enroll and for advance premium tax credit payments before counting a month as a "coverage month" for tax credit purposes. Verification includes household income, family size, immigration status, and other relevant data, with a pre-enrollment verification process required by August 1 for the subsequent plan year. These requirements apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2027.

**Sec. 71304**
Premium tax credits are disallowed for coverage enrolled in during a special enrollment period if the enrollment is based solely on the relationship of expected household income to the poverty line and not due to a qualifying life event. This restriction applies to plan years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71305**
The limitation on the recapture of advance payments of the premium tax credit is eliminated, meaning individuals may be required to repay excess advance payments regardless of their income level. This change applies to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71306**
The safe harbor allowing high-deductible health plans to cover telehealth and remote care services without a deductible is made permanent. This amendment to section 223(c)(2)(E) of the Internal Revenue Code applies to plan years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 71307**
Bronze and catastrophic health plans available through Exchanges are explicitly treated as high-deductible health plans for purposes of Health Savings Account (HSA) eligibility. This amendment to section 223(c)(2) of the Internal Revenue Code applies to months beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71308**
Direct primary care service arrangements, defined as arrangements for primary care services provided by primary care practitioners for a fixed periodic fee not exceeding $150 per month (or $300 for families), are not treated as health plans that disqualify individuals from HSA eligibility. Fees for these arrangements are treated as qualified medical expenses. These changes apply to months beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 71401**
The Rural Health Transformation Program is established, appropriating $10 billion annually for fiscal years 2026 through 2030 to the Administrator of the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services for allotments to states. States must submit applications by December 31, 2025, detailing plans to improve rural health access, outcomes, and financial solvency, and must use funds for at least three specified activities such as workforce recruitment or technology adoption. Allotments are distributed 50 percent equally and 50 percent based on rural population and facility factors, with no state matching funds required. $200,000,000 is appropriated for fiscal year 2025 for implementation.

**Sec. 72001**
The statutory limit on the public debt of the United States is increased by $5,000,000,000,000. This modification applies to the limitation under section 3101(b) of title 31 of the United States Code.

**Sec. 73001**
Federal funds cannot be used to pay unemployment compensation benefits to individuals whose wages during their base period equaled or exceeded $1,000,000. Applicants must self-certify their wage eligibility, and state agencies must verify wage data and recover overpayments from ineligible recipients. This prohibition applies to weeks of unemployment beginning on or after the date of enactment. [end of text]


